      "sample_index": 5,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the relationship between angles and their measures. Let's break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Define the variables:**\n   Let \\( x \\) be the measure of the angle in degrees.\n\n2. **Understand the complement:**\n   The complement of an angle is what, when added to the angle, equals 90 degrees. Therefore, the measure of the complement of \\( x \\) is \\( 90^\\circ - x \\).\n\n3. **Set up the equation:**\n   According to the problem, the measure of the angle is double the measure of its complement. This gives us the equation:\n   \\[\n   x = 2(90^\\circ - x)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solve the equation:**\n   First, distribute the 2 on the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   x = 180^\\circ - 2x\n   \\]\n   Next, add \\( 2x \\) to both sides to combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   x + 2x = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Simplify:\n   \\[\n   3x = 180^\\circ\n   \\]\n   Finally, divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} = 60^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   The measure of the angle is \\( 60^\\circ \\).\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{60^\\circ}\n\\]",
